WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.494
Lesson nine.

00:00:01.494 --> 00:00:06.241
The champion scores well, but it never puts one foot in front of the other.

00:00:06.241 --> 00:00:10.315
Let's look at why, and test a network built to take turns.

00:00:10.915 --> 00:00:12.231
Watch the feet.

00:00:12.231 --> 00:00:15.206
The line below shows which foot is in front.

00:00:15.206 --> 00:00:17.437
Above the line, foot one leads.

00:00:17.437 --> 00:00:19.325
Below it, foot two leads.

00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:22.657
A normal walk crosses the line on every step.

00:00:23.257 --> 00:00:25.364
This walker never crosses it.

00:00:25.364 --> 00:00:29.693
On a hundred tracks, foot two leads one hundred percent of the time.

00:00:29.693 --> 00:00:33.110
Its touchdowns still alternate, so it isn't hopping.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.827
It's skipping, with one leg always reaching forward.

00:00:37.427 --> 00:00:39.801
Why did evolution keep this gait?

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:44.261
The reward pays for distance, not for style, and the skip earns well.

00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:49.936
To switch, the walker would have to pass through clumsy in between gaits that score worse.

00:00:49.936 --> 00:00:53.479
CMA-ES searches nearby, so it stays where it is.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.321
So instead of asking nicely, change the network.

00:00:57.321 --> 00:00:59.869
One controller now drives both legs.

00:00:59.869 --> 00:01:04.095
Each leg sees the body, its own leg, the other leg and the lidar.

00:01:04.095 --> 00:01:07.627
Leg two uses the same weights, with the legs swapped.

00:01:07.627 --> 00:01:10.706
Neither leg can become a permanent prop.

00:01:11.306 --> 00:01:15.519
The controller also gets a clock, a rhythm whose speed it learns.

00:01:15.519 --> 00:01:18.347
Leg two's clock runs half a cycle behind.

00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:22.329
With no shift, both legs would move together, which is a hop.

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:25.111
With half a cycle, they take turns.

00:01:25.711 --> 00:01:32.633
On top, a small training bonus for every step where the front foot changes and the body moves forward.

00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:35.702
Reported scores stay the raw reward.

00:01:36.302 --> 00:01:37.434
The result.

00:01:37.434 --> 00:01:42.079
The new walker changes its leading foot only about zero times per run.

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:45.536
In this run, it didn't clearly learn to walk.

00:01:46.136 --> 00:01:53.515
On the same hundred tracks, it scores two hundred and thirty six, against two hundred and seventy for the skipping walker.

00:01:53.515 --> 00:01:56.808
That's significantly lower, by thirty four points.

00:01:56.808 --> 00:01:59.889
It falls on eleven tracks, against seven.

00:02:00.489 --> 00:02:04.681
The lesson: evolution finds what you reward, the nearest way.

00:02:04.681 --> 00:02:08.809
If you care how it's done, build it into the structure.
